            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Divides a rectangle into a grid of cells and counts how many of a set of points fall in each. The counts make a picture of where the points gather: an occupancy map.\n\nFeed it the floor positions of performers over a scene and it shows where they spent their time; a hand's positions over a phrase, the reach of a gesture; any two features of a stream against each other, how they go together.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.histogram2d <bins: int> <x min> <x max> <y min> <y max>\nDefaults: 10 x 10 bins, auto range. Giving the four limits turns auto range off.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.histogram2d 20 -2 2 -2 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoints:\nThe points, as rows of x y (an array with 2 columns; any further columns are ignored). Points with nan or infinity are left out. Every input is counted and sent.\n\nbins (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many cells along each side.\n\nx min, x max, y min, y max (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe rectangle the grid covers, when auto range is off. Points outside it are not counted.\n\nauto range:\nAn option, under the node. On, the grid spans the points' own extent, so it moves with them; off, it stays fixed, so one picture can be compared with the next.\n\ndensity:\nAn option, under the node. On, the counts are scaled so they add up to 1 over the area: comparable between sets of different sizes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncounts:\nThe grid of counts, as a picture: one row per y cell and one column per x cell. Row 0 is the lowest y, which a heat_map draws at the top.\n\nx edges, y edges:\nWhere the cells start and end along each side. Sent before the counts.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a thousand points, each coordinate the sum of four random numbers, so they pile up around the middle (2, 2) and thin out towards the edges. Counted in a 16 x 16 grid, they make a soft blob, redrawn twice a second.\n\nBottom: four points in a 2 x 2 grid over 0 to 2. Three have y below 1 (the top row):\none with x below 1 and two above it, so 1 2. One has y above 1 and x below 1: 1 0.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.histogram counts along one line. np.digitize gives each value's bin. heat_map draws the counts.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Divides a rectangle into a grid of cells and counts how many of a set of points fall in each. The counts make a picture of where the points gather: an occupancy map.\n\nFeed it the floor positions of performers over a scene and it shows where they spent their time; a hand's positions over a phrase, the reach of a gesture; any two features of a stream against each other, how they go together.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.histogram2d <bins: int> <x min> <x max> <y min> <y max>\nDefaults: 10 x 10 bins, auto range. Giving the four limits turns auto range off.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.histogram2d 20 -2 2 -2 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoints:\nThe points, as rows of x y (an array with 2 columns; any further columns are ignored). Points with nan or infinity are left out. Every input is counted and sent.\n\nbins (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many cells along each side.\n\nx min, x max, y min, y max (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe rectangle the grid covers, when auto range is off. Points outside it are not counted.\n\nauto range:\nAn option, under the node. On, the grid spans the points' own extent, so it moves with them; off, it stays fixed, so one picture can be compared with the next.\n\ndensity:\nAn option, under the node. On, the counts are scaled so they add up to 1 over the area: comparable between sets of different sizes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncounts:\nThe grid of counts, as a picture: one row per y cell and one column per x cell. Row 0 is the lowest y, which a heat_map draws at the top.\n\nx edges, y edges:\nWhere the cells start and end along each side. Sent before the counts.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a thousand points, each coordinate the sum of four random numbers, so they pile up around the middle (2, 2) and thin out towards the edges. Counted in a 16 x 16 grid, they make a soft blob, redrawn twice a second.\n\nBottom: four points in a 2 x 2 grid over 0 to 2. Three have y below 1 (the top row):\none with x below 1 and two above it, so 1 2. One has y above 1 and x below 1: 1 0.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.histogram counts along one line. np.digitize gives each value's bin. heat_map draws the counts."
        },
